WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.299
John: Hey, I'm not a hippie I'm not right wing.

00:00:03.319 --> 00:00:05.810
Anything you've read about me is mostly bullshit.

00:00:05.830 --> 00:00:06.830
[Laughter]

00:00:06.830 --> 00:00:09.869
John: So hopefully you'll form your own opinions based in my

00:00:09.889 --> 00:00:16.079
talk today.

00:00:16.099 --> 00:00:17.099
John:

00:00:16.349 --> 00:00:17.349
[Pause]

00:00:16.349 --> 00:00:20.539
This talk sometimes takes an hour, so I'm told the people at

00:00:20.559 --> 00:00:21.559
Stanford are really smart.

00:00:21.559 --> 00:00:24.809
So I'm gonna go really fast assuming you're all really quick

00:00:24.799 --> 00:00:28.469
studies and that will save more time for Q and A.

00:00:28.489 --> 00:00:32.039
So I do think we need new paradigms for the way we think

00:00:32.059 --> 00:00:35.629
about business, and the way we think about capitalism.

00:00:35.649 --> 00:00:38.359
Someday, historians are gonna look back on the 20th century,

00:00:38.379 --> 00:00:42.759
and their gonna see that a great war was fought between two

00:00:42.780 --> 00:00:44.909
different philosophy's of economics.

00:00:44.929 --> 00:00:46.299
Socialism and Capitalism.

00:00:46.320 --> 00:00:47.320
[Sneeze]

00:00:46.320 --> 00:00:46.520
[]

00:00:46.320 --> 00:00:51.599
John: Capitalism won that competition.

00:00:51.619 --> 00:00:53.749
Every place that it had

00:00:53.769 --> 00:00:54.769
[Sneeze]

00:00:53.769 --> 00:00:56.889
John: direct competition, whether it be United States vs.

00:00:56.909 --> 00:01:03.949
Soviet Union, or South Korea vs. North Korea, or East

00:01:03.969 --> 00:01:07.299
Germany vs. West Germany etc, etc, etc,

00:01:07.319 --> 00:01:12.909
However, it did not capture the minds of the intellectuals

00:01:12.929 --> 00:01:14.810
or the heats of the people.

00:01:14.829 --> 00:01:17.599
Capitalism was still greatly mistrusted.

00:01:17.619 --> 00:01:20.799
And corporations who I believe are the most influential

00:01:20.819 --> 00:01:24.689
institution of the world are widely perceived as greedy,

00:01:24.709 --> 00:01:26.140
selfish and exploitative.

00:01:26.159 --> 00:01:28.890
And they are certainly not worthy of any trust.

00:01:28.909 --> 00:01:32.569
You can see this very clearly with deep recession that we've

00:01:32.589 --> 00:01:37.090
been into, the financial crisis has been demagoged as the

00:01:37.109 --> 00:01:42.329
fault of greed and corporations out of control.

00:01:42.349 --> 00:01:45.799
Cap -- Another example of Capitalism not working.

00:01:45.819 --> 00:01:49.929
And there's been no blame for bad government policies, bad

00:01:49.949 --> 00:01:53.640
monetary policies, bad regulations.

00:01:53.659 --> 00:01:56.359
And I would argue that corporations and capitalism have

00:01:56.379 --> 00:01:59.060
serious branding problems.

00:01:59.079 --> 00:02:02.019
They are not branded very well, they don't market themselves

00:02:02.039 --> 00:02:06.030
very well in the PR side of things.

00:02:06.049 --> 00:02:10.179
And what I see happening is that even though capitalism won

00:02:10.199 --> 00:02:13.399
the battles in the 20, 20th century that were rapidly moving

00:02:13.419 --> 00:02:18.319
back toward more socialism, more progressivism.

00:02:18.340 --> 00:02:20.840
And I'm gonna argue that there's another way.

00:02:20.359 --> 00:02:23.879
That conscious leadership conscious business and conscious

00:02:23.900 --> 00:02:26.389
capitalism is what we should be doing.

00:02:26.409 --> 00:02:31.699
So, conscious capitalism is based on four primary principles

00:02:31.719 --> 00:02:32.719
of economics.

00:02:32.719 --> 00:02:34.719
Secure property rights, freedom of contracts, freedom of

00:02:34.739 --> 00:02:37.519
trade and the rule of law.

00:02:37.539 --> 00:02:42.049
Conscious capitalism is equal to these four primary economic

00:02:42.069 --> 00:02:46.239
principles plus conscious business.

00:02:46.259 --> 00:02:49.909
Which leaves the question, what then is conscious business?

00:02:49.929 --> 00:02:51.329
Conscious business is based on?

00:02:51.349 --> 00:02:53.599
It's going in and out, in and out.

00:02:53.319 --> 00:02:54.319
[Louder with Mike Feedback]

00:02:53.319 --> 00:02:55.279
John: Maybe I'll talk, whoa.

00:02:55.299 --> 00:02:56.299
[Laughter]

00:02:55.299 --> 00:03:03.219
John: Conscious business is based on these three primary

00:03:03.239 --> 00:03:05.739
principles of enterprise which is what I'm gonna primarily

00:03:05.719 --> 00:03:06.719
talk about.

00:03:06.739 --> 00:03:11.019
The enterprise has the potential to have a higher purpose

00:03:11.039 --> 00:03:14.319
beyond merely maximizing profit and shareholder value.

00:03:14.340 --> 00:03:19.689
The enterprise is managed to optimize value simultaneously

00:03:19.709 --> 00:03:22.839
for all of the interdependent stakeholders.

00:03:22.859 --> 00:03:26.099
And then the leadership of the organization is conscious

00:03:26.120 --> 00:03:29.449
organization that serves the enterprise and the

00:03:29.469 --> 00:03:32.619
stakeholders.

00:03:32.639 --> 00:03:35.889
If you go to a cocktail party and you ask people what the

00:03:35.659 --> 00:03:39.149
purpose of a business is -- do this experiment sometime --

00:03:39.169 --> 00:03:43.579
you'll find 99% of the time people will say, "what do you

00:03:43.599 --> 00:03:46.349
mean what the purpose of business is, purpose of business is

00:03:46.349 --> 00:03:47.849
to make money. Everybody knows that."

00:03:47.859 --> 00:03:50.409
But that's kind of a curious answer.

00:03:50.429 --> 00:03:53.429
Because if you ask what the purpose of a doctor is, people

00:03:53.209 --> 00:03:55.959
aren't gonna tell you, "Well, everybody knows the purpose of

00:03:55.449 --> 00:03:56.949
a doctor is to make money."

00:03:56.849 --> 00:04:00.349
Or if you ask what the purpose of a teacher is, no one says

00:04:00.129 --> 00:04:02.129
"Well teacher's purpose is to make money."

00:04:02.089 --> 00:04:05.169
Purpose of a doctor is to heal people, purpose of a teacher,

00:04:05.189 --> 00:04:08.109
to educate people, purpose of an architect, design

00:04:08.129 --> 00:04:09.789
buildings.

00:04:09.810 --> 00:04:13.889
Why is it that we say the purpose of a business is to make

00:04:13.909 --> 00:04:15.759
money?

00:04:15.780 --> 00:04:21.600
And who defines what the purpose of a business is anyway?

00:04:21.620 --> 00:04:25.509
I'll argue that the entrepreneur that creates the business

00:04:25.529 --> 00:04:29.180
in the first place is the one who has the right to find what

00:04:29.199 --> 00:04:30.829
the purpose of that business is.

00:04:30.849 --> 00:04:34.160
And I have known hundreds, hundreds of entrepreneurs in my

00:04:34.180 --> 00:04:35.180
lifetime.

00:04:35.180 --> 00:04:38.189
And with only a few exceptions have they told me that their

00:04:38.209 --> 00:04:42.519
primary purpose was to make as much money as possible.

00:04:42.539 --> 00:04:44.899
They had multiplicity of reasons why they started their

00:04:44.919 --> 00:04:45.919
business.

00:04:45.919 --> 00:04:49.169
And sure they wanted to make money, but that wasn't what was

00:04:48.939 --> 00:04:50.639
driving them.

00:04:50.659 --> 00:04:54.159
I had the privilege to meet Bill Gates a year or so ago, and

00:04:54.139 --> 00:04:56.139
I got to talking about this with Bill.

00:04:56.139 --> 00:04:59.959
And believe it or not, he didn't start Microsoft to become

00:04:59.979 --> 00:05:01.540
the wealthiest man in the world.

00:05:01.560 --> 00:05:04.769
He was on fire about software.

00:05:04.789 --> 00:05:08.500
He had a vision of everyone having their own personal

00:05:08.519 --> 00:05:09.610
computer.

00:05:09.629 --> 00:05:11.639
And that was an incredibly revolutionary idea.

00:05:11.659 --> 00:05:14.629
Let me ask -- quick poll here, how many people have their

00:05:14.649 --> 00:05:15.649
own personal computer?

00:05:15.649 --> 00:05:16.649
[Audience Mumbling]

00:05:15.649 --> 00:05:21.170
John: That's why Bill Gates is the richest man in the world,

00:05:21.189 --> 00:05:24.590
because he had a vision about that and he pursued it with a

00:05:24.610 --> 00:05:26.860
single mindedness and a passion.

00:05:26.879 --> 00:05:30.259
And the rest so to speak is history.

00:05:30.279 --> 00:05:32.730
Entrepreneurs create the original purpose of business.

00:05:32.750 --> 00:05:37.370
And in the 21st century, entrepreneurs of the conscious

00:05:37.389 --> 00:05:40.800
business are also entrepreneurs of meaning.

00:05:40.819 --> 00:05:44.530
They must determine the meaning, and they must interpret it,

00:05:44.550 --> 00:05:46.660
and they must explain it.

00:05:46.680 --> 00:05:49.180
And what I found very interesting is that although the

00:05:48.759 --> 00:05:51.040
entrepreneur may create the original purpose of the

00:05:51.060 --> 00:05:56.949
business, it's the stakeholders that ultimately evolve the

00:05:56.969 --> 00:05:59.170
purpose of the business over time.

00:05:59.189 --> 00:06:03.850
And these communities of stakeholders, interacting with the

00:06:03.870 --> 00:06:08.110
enterprise, the businesses purpose continues to evolve.

00:06:08.129 --> 00:06:12.079
And the businesses that flourish over the long term will

00:06:12.099 --> 00:06:15.170
continue to get to either -- you can do a metaphor of higher

00:06:15.189 --> 00:06:18.660
and higher, or deeper and deeper in terms of the meaning of

00:06:18.680 --> 00:06:19.889
what their purpose is.

00:06:19.909 --> 00:06:24.579
To higher levels of complexity and meaning.

00:06:24.599 --> 00:06:28.879
The great companies in this world have great purposes.

00:06:28.899 --> 00:06:32.680
Think about the companies that you most admire, scratch the

00:06:32.699 --> 00:06:36.449
surface and you will find they are trying to fulfill a

00:06:36.469 --> 00:06:37.959
deeper or higher purpose.

00:06:37.979 --> 00:06:40.479
They're not always completely conscious of it, but it

00:06:40.490 --> 00:06:42.959
resonates in their very DNA.

00:06:42.979 --> 00:06:47.430
And stealing from Plato, the deepest purposes the he was

00:06:47.449 --> 00:06:50.410
able to articulate, the good, the true and the beautiful, I

00:06:50.430 --> 00:06:51.649
added on heroic.

00:06:51.669 --> 00:06:58.740
These four categories are sufficient to explain at the most

00:06:58.759 --> 00:07:02.300
abstract level what the potential deeper purpose is that

00:07:02.319 --> 00:07:06.810
each business can try to manifest in the world.

00:07:06.829 --> 00:07:09.920
If you think about a business that's serving the good, or

00:07:09.939 --> 00:07:12.889
fulfilling -- pursuing the good, one of my favorite examples

00:07:12.909 --> 00:07:18.230
is Southwest airlines which is a tremendously -- very, I

00:07:18.250 --> 00:07:20.019
think very much a conscious business.

00:07:20.039 --> 00:07:22.539
And definitely is pursuing a deeper purpose of service to

00:07:22.250 --> 00:07:23.250
others.

00:07:23.250 --> 00:07:27.259
Nordstrom's another good example, their terms of the true.

00:07:27.279 --> 00:07:30.550
Think about how exciting it must be to discover something

00:07:30.569 --> 00:07:35.600
that no one has ever known before, that advances humanity.

00:07:35.620 --> 00:07:39.279
And yet that's what companies like Google have done, or

00:07:39.299 --> 00:07:42.829
Intel, or Genentech.

00:07:42.849 --> 00:07:44.849
Those are three of my favorite examples there.

00:07:44.500 --> 00:07:47.250
How about the beautiful, what's beautiful have to do with

00:07:46.250 --> 00:07:47.250
business?

00:07:47.250 --> 00:07:49.750
In a sense a business that strives for excellence, because

00:07:49.729 --> 00:07:52.889
excellence is inherently beautiful.

00:07:52.909 --> 00:07:54.579
How about Apple?

00:07:54.599 --> 00:07:56.360
Their technology is beautiful.

00:07:56.379 --> 00:08:01.459
I think IPhones, IMacs, IPods are beautiful technologies.

00:08:01.479 --> 00:08:04.050
Warren Buffet, what he's done with Brookshire Hathaway has

00:08:04.069 --> 00:08:07.230
made investing into something I think is an art form, it's

00:08:07.250 --> 00:08:08.600
beautiful.

00:08:08.620 --> 00:08:11.839
We talk about the heroic courage to do what it right, to

00:08:11.859 --> 00:08:13.109
change or improve the world.

00:08:13.109 --> 00:08:16.930
Bill Gates at Microsoft and now with the Gates foundation.

00:08:16.949 --> 00:08:21.610
Muhammad Yunus with the Grameem, Grameem banks are two of my

00:08:21.629 --> 00:08:22.629
favorite examples.

00:08:22.629 --> 00:08:28.930
I call this the paradox of profits.

00:08:28.949 --> 00:08:34.009
And the best way to understand it is through the metaphor

00:08:34.029 --> 00:08:36.360
and the analogy of happiness.

00:08:36.379 --> 00:08:39.929
I'm now old enough to be able to speak with some authority

00:08:39.949 --> 00:08:41.590
about happiness.

00:08:41.610 --> 00:08:44.909
And what I've found is that happiness is a bi-product of

00:08:44.929 --> 00:08:46.110
other things.

00:08:46.129 --> 00:08:51.149
That people who tell you that the purpose of their -- that -

00:08:51.169 --> 00:08:54.679
- purpose of their life is to be happy probably aren't

00:08:54.699 --> 00:08:56.199
gonna, aren't gonna find it.

00:08:56.100 --> 00:09:00.220
Cuz happiness is a -- people that are obsessed with finding

00:09:00.240 --> 00:09:02.820
their own happiness tend to be sort of self involved

00:09:02.840 --> 00:09:03.840
narcissists.

00:09:03.840 --> 00:09:04.840
[Audience Chuckles]

00:09:03.840 --> 00:09:06.960
John: And that's a very bad strategy.

00:09:06.980 --> 00:09:10.100
It does not lead to ultimate happiness or fulfillment.

00:09:10.120 --> 00:09:13.620
Rather having a deeper purpose, service, striving for

00:09:13.639 --> 00:09:17.600
excellence, growing as a hum -- individual, friendship,

00:09:17.620 --> 00:09:19.679
love, generosity.

00:09:19.699 --> 00:09:23.139
These are the type of virtues that when practiced, lead to

00:09:23.159 --> 00:09:24.679
happiness.

00:09:24.699 --> 00:09:29.299
Similarly, I believe that profits are best realized when

00:09:29.319 --> 00:09:32.080
they're not made the primary goal of the business.

00:09:32.100 --> 00:09:35.220
That they are also a bi-product of deeper purpose, great

00:09:35.240 --> 00:09:38.759
products and services, customer satisfaction, employee

00:09:38.779 --> 00:09:41.269
happiness and social and environmental responsibility.

00:09:41.289 --> 00:09:47.750
The metaphors that we use to think about business are not

00:09:47.769 --> 00:09:49.830
good metaphors.

00:09:49.850 --> 00:09:54.360
One, going back to Adam Smith and the industrial revolution

00:09:54.379 --> 00:09:56.809
in terms of business being like a machine.

00:09:56.829 --> 00:10:00.189
You put in land, labor and capital, put it on the assembly

00:10:00.209 --> 00:10:02.700
line and do it, profits spit out.

00:10:02.720 --> 00:10:06.250
The Darwinian models of survival of the fittest.

00:10:06.269 --> 00:10:07.750
War metaphors,

00:10:07.769 --> 00:10:08.769
[Harsh Voice]

00:10:07.769 --> 00:10:10.740
John: We're gonna crush our competitors and roll 'em up.

00:10:10.759 --> 00:10:11.759
[Normal voice]

00:10:10.759 --> 00:10:15.909
John: I don't think those are great metaphors for really

00:10:15.929 --> 00:10:17.740
understanding business.

00:10:17.759 --> 00:10:20.159
Metaphors do determine how we think about things.

00:10:20.179 --> 00:10:23.429
We need to be careful in terms of the metaphors that we use

00:10:22.509 --> 00:10:24.250
to think about anything.

00:10:24.269 --> 00:10:27.519
I'm gonna argue that a good metaphor to use in the 21st

00:10:27.439 --> 00:10:31.870
century is that of a complex adaptive self-organizing system

00:10:31.889 --> 00:10:35.049
that creates value for all of the inter-dependant

00:10:35.069 --> 00:10:38.789
constituencies or stakeholders.

00:10:38.809 --> 00:10:41.019
What is conscious leadership?

00:10:41.039 --> 00:10:43.559
Conscious leadership fills the deeper purpose of the

00:10:43.579 --> 00:10:46.269
organization, create value for the inter-dependant

00:10:46.289 --> 00:10:50.090
stakeholders, and to be a servant leader to the enterprise.

00:10:50.110 --> 00:10:51.860
What do I mean by servant leader?

00:10:51.829 --> 00:10:55.259
It means you're serving the enterprise and its purpose.

00:10:55.279 --> 00:10:58.529
You're no there trying to line your own pockets and see how

00:10:58.100 --> 00:11:00.210
much money you can make.

00:11:00.230 --> 00:11:03.879
The true transforming of leadership is serving that

00:11:03.899 --> 00:11:05.069
organization.

00:11:05.089 --> 00:11:07.839
So adopting the metaphor of a servant leader is a very

00:11:07.750 --> 00:11:09.569
appropriate metaphor.

00:11:09.589 --> 00:11:12.500
Creating value for the entire interdependent system -- I

00:11:12.519 --> 00:11:16.519
will argue today and this is my radical idea, that that will

00:11:16.539 --> 00:11:20.169
also simultaneously result in the highest long-term profits

00:11:20.189 --> 00:11:21.189
and shareholder value.

00:11:21.189 --> 00:11:25.460
I'm gonna argue that Whole Foods is a conscious business.

00:11:25.480 --> 00:11:28.230
There are some other good examples right here, the only one

00:11:28.089 --> 00:11:30.899
I understand really well from the inside out is Whole Foods,

00:11:30.919 --> 00:11:34.840
so I'm gonna use that as my primary example.

00:11:34.860 --> 00:11:37.860
Here's a simple model that we use to think about our

00:11:37.509 --> 00:11:38.610
business.

00:11:38.629 --> 00:11:41.429
It starts with our mission, why we exist?

00:11:41.449 --> 00:11:43.029
What our deeper purpose is.

00:11:43.049 --> 00:11:46.730
The core values, they're at the center, they're at the heart

00:11:46.750 --> 00:11:47.750
of everything we do.

00:11:47.750 --> 00:11:51.399
And they're radiating out from that heart like, like spokes

00:11:51.419 --> 00:11:55.080
on a wheel, are the major interdependent stakeholders.

00:11:55.100 --> 00:11:58.100
The team members, the suppliers, the customers, the

00:11:58.120 --> 00:12:03.110
investors and the community and the environment.

00:12:03.129 --> 00:12:06.870
This models over simplified because a web of all those

00:12:06.889 --> 00:12:09.330
stakeholders interacting with each other would be more

00:12:09.350 --> 00:12:12.350
appropriate, but I haven't figured out how to model that in

00:12:12.100 --> 00:12:14.569
a simplistic way yet.

00:12:14.589 --> 00:12:18.720
But simply, when you allow these stakeholders to -- when you

00:12:18.740 --> 00:12:23.000
manage to value for all of them at the same time, you end up

00:12:23.019 --> 00:12:25.659
in a upward spiral.

00:12:25.679 --> 00:12:28.490
So in sense this is a circle, but a more appropriate

00:12:28.509 --> 00:12:31.600
metaphor would be to show this in an upward spiral of

00:12:31.620 --> 00:12:37.699
continued value creation.

00:12:37.719 --> 00:12:44.340
So, conscious leadership focuses on synergies, not on the

00:12:44.360 --> 00:12:45.460
trade offs.

00:12:45.480 --> 00:12:47.980
I'm heading off a question that I will get.

00:12:47.980 --> 00:12:50.730
What about the trade offs and the conflicts that you have

00:12:50.519 --> 00:12:51.519
between the various stakeholders?

00:12:51.519 --> 00:12:54.519
The more you give the employees, the less money you have to

00:12:54.039 --> 00:12:55.039
give the investors.

00:12:55.039 --> 00:12:57.539
The more money you give away to not for profit

00:12:57.539 --> 00:12:59.789
organizations, then the less money you have for shareholder

00:12:59.199 --> 00:13:00.199
values.

00:13:00.199 --> 00:13:03.199
The higher you pay your workers, the less money there'll be

00:13:02.459 --> 00:13:03.459
for investors.

00:13:03.459 --> 00:13:08.699
And so forth and so on, as if a Hobbs war against -- the war

00:13:08.719 --> 00:13:11.189
against, all against all is in play here.

00:13:11.209 --> 00:13:15.100
That there's this ongoing struggle between the stakeholders.

00:13:15.120 --> 00:13:18.069
The conscious leader sees the inner-dependencies, and sees

00:13:18.089 --> 00:13:19.240
the relationships.

00:13:19.259 --> 00:13:22.669
If you look for trade-offs, you can find them.

00:13:22.689 --> 00:13:25.610
If you look for synergies, you can find them.

00:13:25.629 --> 00:13:29.129
The conscious leader looks for the synergies, looks for the

00:13:29.149 --> 00:13:34.360
strategies that will create value, simultaneously for all of

00:13:34.379 --> 00:13:36.759
the inner-dependant stakeholders.

00:13:36.779 --> 00:13:40.029
One's creative mind does not need to think in terms of the

00:13:39.969 --> 00:13:43.569
zero sum game, I win you lose, you lose, I win.

00:13:43.589 --> 00:13:46.769
We can create win-win-win strategies.

00:13:46.789 --> 00:13:50.509
That is the beauty of capitalism, that is its potential, to

00:13:50.529 --> 00:13:53.279
create value for all of the ones that are voluntarily

00:13:53.299 --> 00:13:55.569
changing with the business.

00:13:55.589 --> 00:13:59.539
The human mind is infinitely creative.

00:13:59.559 --> 00:14:03.090
Beginning to think this way creates new mental pathways and

00:14:03.110 --> 00:14:07.750
unleashes human creativity in unexpected ways.

00:14:07.769 --> 00:14:10.620
It will be the creative entrepreneurs and the conscious

00:14:10.639 --> 00:14:14.480
capitalist who will solve the problems that the world faces

00:14:14.500 --> 00:14:15.990
in the 21st century.

00:14:16.009 --> 00:14:20.220
It's going to be that individual creativity applied

00:14:20.240 --> 00:14:24.539
collaboratively through enterprise that will solve most of

00:14:24.559 --> 00:14:28.399
the world's problems.

00:14:28.419 --> 00:14:30.959
What is Whole Foods deepest purpose?

00:14:30.979 --> 00:14:33.479
I used to believe for many years Whole Foods' deepest

00:14:33.339 --> 00:14:34.419
purpose was the good.

00:14:34.439 --> 00:14:38.539
We're in the service business, we're to serve other people.

00:14:38.559 --> 00:14:41.809
And then as I did this talk around Whole Foods, and I would

00:14:41.809 --> 00:14:44.559
instead of giving the answer, I would poll the team members.

00:14:44.329 --> 00:14:49.120
I would say well --I'd explain the four deepest, highest

00:14:49.139 --> 00:14:52.929
purposes and then I'd take a poll and I'd ask the team

00:14:52.949 --> 00:14:55.699
members to tell me, "What do you think the deepest purpose

00:14:55.699 --> 00:14:57.449
or highest purpose of Whole Foods is?"

00:14:57.449 --> 00:14:59.149
And I got an astounding answer.

00:14:59.169 --> 00:15:02.419
And it wasn't -- I got answers for all of them, but about

00:15:02.439 --> 00:15:05.480
75% of the team members tell me that Whole Foods' highest

00:15:05.500 --> 00:15:07.250
purpose is heroic.

00:15:07.269 --> 00:15:10.679
To, to do what is right and to change and improve the world.

00:15:10.699 --> 00:15:12.449
And I used to argue with them.

00:15:11.779 --> 00:15:15.279
I used to say "No it's not, I created Whole Foods, that's

00:15:14.219 --> 00:15:17.719
not, that's not why we did it. Its service, it's about love.

00:15:17.339 --> 00:15:17.590
"

00:15:17.610 --> 00:15:18.649
So service to others.

00:15:18.669 --> 00:15:20.919
No John, that's not what it's about.

00:15:20.639 --> 00:15:24.139
So I had this argument, and then I'd go around all over the

00:15:23.559 --> 00:15:26.230
country, and finally I surrendered and said, "You know what,

00:15:26.250 --> 00:15:29.610
this is a great example of the enterprise out growing its

00:15:29.629 --> 00:15:32.259
founder and moving in its own direction."

00:15:32.279 --> 00:15:35.269
Just like a parent raises children, but eventually the child

00:15:35.289 --> 00:15:38.459
fulfills its own destiny, often times in a direction

00:15:38.479 --> 00:15:41.789
different from what the parents would aspire to.

00:15:41.809 --> 00:15:43.980
So I've embraced this myself.

00:15:44.000 --> 00:15:47.000
These are the seven core values of Whole Foods that are at

00:15:46.370 --> 00:15:48.120
the center of everything that we do.

00:15:48.110 --> 00:15:54.009
Just real quickly, in terms of those core values.

00:15:54.029 --> 00:15:56.029
Selling the highest quality natural and organic foods

00:15:55.719 --> 00:15:56.719
available.

00:15:56.719 --> 00:16:00.219
We're very proud of the fact that we've been able to improve

00:15:58.929 --> 00:16:01.419
the health, wellness and longevity of literally millions of

00:16:01.439 --> 00:16:02.439
people.

00:16:02.439 --> 00:16:05.759
We've proven that good health and pleasurable eating are

00:16:05.779 --> 00:16:08.639
compatible goals.

00:16:08.659 --> 00:16:12.529
Alternatives to the degradation of -- industrial degradation

00:16:12.549 --> 00:16:13.589
of our food.

00:16:13.609 --> 00:16:16.189
And we've educated, or beginning to educate our stakeholders

00:16:16.209 --> 00:16:19.559
in terms of what healthy eating's all about.

00:16:19.579 --> 00:16:22.329
How many of you hope to start your own business someday?

00:16:22.279 --> 00:16:23.279
[Pause]

00:16:22.279 --> 00:16:25.649
John: Wow, great.

00:16:25.669 --> 00:16:27.669
I'm giving to you the biggest secret.

00:16:27.289 --> 00:16:29.049
It's all about the customers.

00:16:29.069 --> 00:16:30.069
[Audience mumbling]

00:16:29.069 --> 00:16:34.159
John: You'd be surprised how few businesses really

00:16:34.179 --> 00:16:35.579
understand that.

00:16:35.599 --> 00:16:38.620
And so you have to design your business model first and

00:16:38.639 --> 00:16:40.289
foremost around the customers.

00:16:40.309 --> 00:16:43.049
Because if you do that, you will probably win.

00:16:43.069 --> 00:16:46.139
Because chances are your competitors will not be as

00:16:46.159 --> 00:16:48.230
committed to that as you will be.

00:16:48.250 --> 00:16:51.029
So they're the most important stakeholder.

00:16:51.049 --> 00:16:52.879
No customers, no business.

00:16:52.899 --> 00:16:56.789
Customers always exchange voluntarily, they're not coerced,

00:16:56.809 --> 00:16:59.179
they trade voluntarily with the enterprise.

00:16:59.199 --> 00:17:01.949
Therefore you have to treat them as ends in themselves, and

00:17:01.629 --> 00:17:02.629
not as means.

00:17:02.629 --> 00:17:05.380
We designed our business model at Whole Foods so that our

00:17:05.379 --> 00:17:07.629
team members are completely empowered to do whatever it

00:17:07.319 --> 00:17:09.940
takes to satisfy and delight the customers.

00:17:09.960 --> 00:17:13.019
Of course the team members think they're #1.

00:17:13.039 --> 00:17:15.789
Everyone's' disappointed when I tell them they're just the #

00:17:15.430 --> 00:17:16.680
2 stakeholder to the customers.

00:17:16.680 --> 00:17:18.930
These are some of the innovations we've done.

00:17:18.680 --> 00:17:21.930
It'd be interesting to go into these in some detail but for

00:17:21.710 --> 00:17:23.210
lack of time, I'm not.

00:17:22.960 --> 00:17:24.960
Self managing teams, offered salary information, salary cap

00:17:24.980 --> 00:17:27.539
benefits, insurance for all, stock options for all team

00:17:27.559 --> 00:17:30.240
members, personal wellness accounts, fully paid health

00:17:30.259 --> 00:17:31.349
insurance.

00:17:31.369 --> 00:17:34.990
And Bill said we've been named one of the hundred best

00:17:35.009 --> 00:17:40.710
companies to work for for 13 years in a row, #18 in 2010.

00:17:40.730 --> 00:17:45.619
This is often times by the time I get to this part of the

00:17:45.639 --> 00:17:47.889
presentation people think, "This guys like, he's like

00:17:47.659 --> 00:17:50.399
against profits. He's talkin about all this other stuff."

00:17:50.419 --> 00:17:54.119
But profits are very important, and it's one of the most

00:17:54.139 --> 00:17:58.710
important reasons and functions of the business.

00:17:58.730 --> 00:18:01.789
And that profits are created through voluntary exchange.

00:18:01.809 --> 00:18:04.490
They are not primarily created through the exploitation of

00:18:04.509 --> 00:18:05.759
people.

00:18:05.779 --> 00:18:09.339
It is that voluntary exchange that fundamentally makes

00:18:09.359 --> 00:18:10.359
business ethical.

00:18:10.359 --> 00:18:13.099
No one is forced to trade.

00:18:13.119 --> 00:18:15.619
The customers aren't forced to trade, employees aren't

00:18:15.139 --> 00:18:18.920
forced to trade, suppliers aren't forced to trade, investors

00:18:18.940 --> 00:18:20.190
aren't forced to invest.

00:18:20.019 --> 00:18:22.299
Everyone invests voluntarily.

00:18:22.319 --> 00:18:24.190
That's what makes it inherently ethical.

00:18:24.210 --> 00:18:29.609
Profit are not evil, profits are good.

00:18:29.629 --> 00:18:32.039
Profits create wealth, they create capital and they create

00:18:32.059 --> 00:18:33.059
prosperity.

00:18:33.059 --> 00:18:37.909
Capital is the fuel for all the technological progress the

00:18:37.929 --> 00:18:39.329
world has ever known.

00:18:39.349 --> 00:18:43.549
Without the fuel, there would be no progress.

00:18:43.569 --> 00:18:45.569
Whole Foods has tried to do its part.

00:18:45.429 --> 00:18:47.679
We've been the fastest growing publically traded food

00:18:47.429 --> 00:18:48.970
retailer for the past ten years.

00:18:48.990 --> 00:18:53.519
8 billion in sales, our same store sales have averaged 9%,

00:18:53.539 --> 00:18:55.289
our return on invested capital is 35%.

00:18:55.309 --> 00:19:00.460
EBADA companion or growth rate, 30% a year since we went

00:19:00.480 --> 00:19:05.409
public in 1992, our stocks increased 1400% since our 1992

00:19:05.429 --> 00:19:09.480
IPO, even though it traded down tremendously in 19 -- 2007

00:19:09.500 --> 00:19:14.609
and 2008.

00:19:14.629 --> 00:19:17.629
I say one of the most important functions in business it to

00:19:17.429 --> 00:19:18.559
create prosperity.

00:19:18.579 --> 00:19:22.049
Business has a fundamental responsibility to create

00:19:22.069 --> 00:19:25.710
prosperity for society and indeed for the entire world.

00:19:25.730 --> 00:19:32.349
Do you realize that less than 200 years ago, 85% of the

00:19:32.369 --> 00:19:35.369
people alive on the planet earth lived on less than a dollar

00:19:34.750 --> 00:19:41.250
a day, and that's in today's dollars, 85%!

00:19:41.269 --> 00:19:44.389
We're down to 20%, that's progress.

00:19:44.409 --> 00:19:47.159
That's what business has done, that is what capitalism has

00:19:47.099 --> 00:19:48.099
done.

00:19:48.099 --> 00:19:51.099
No, it's terrible that we still have over a billion people

00:19:51.109 --> 00:19:54.109
on this planet that live on less than a dollar day, but

00:19:53.769 --> 00:19:58.839
that's still only about 20% instead of 85%.

00:19:58.859 --> 00:20:02.819
If we don't screw it up, we will end poverty on the planet

00:20:02.839 --> 00:20:04.409
earth in the 21st century.

00:20:04.429 --> 00:20:06.929
There are people young enough in this audience today that

00:20:06.750 --> 00:20:08.399
will live to see that.

00:20:08.419 --> 00:20:12.279
As Muhammad Yunus says, "Someday, poverty will be something

00:20:12.299 --> 00:20:15.670
people only see in museums."

00:20:15.690 --> 00:20:18.700
This is one of the heroic things that business is doing.

00:20:18.720 --> 00:20:23.480
It is creating wealth, it is creating prosperity and it is

00:20:23.500 --> 00:20:24.889
lessening poverty.

00:20:24.909 --> 00:20:29.799
In the last 15 years alo -- alone in India and Chine,

00:20:29.819 --> 00:20:32.920
literally hundreds of millions of people have been able to

00:20:32.940 --> 00:20:36.200
escape from poverty.

00:20:36.220 --> 00:20:39.250
I do not believe suppliers are supposed to be beaten up, I

00:20:39.269 --> 00:20:41.289
believe they're partners with the business.

00:20:41.309 --> 00:20:45.369
And business has to create win-win relationships with their

00:20:45.389 --> 00:20:46.389
suppliers.

00:20:46.389 --> 00:20:50.259
And I'm very proud of the fact that all these little tiny ma

00:20:50.279 --> 00:20:52.319
and pa suppliers, they're all millionaire's.

00:20:52.339 --> 00:20:55.839
Now most of them sold out, and it's sort of sad they sold

00:20:54.909 --> 00:20:58.799
out to big corporations, but I'm happy they got wealthy, and

00:20:58.819 --> 00:21:01.079
Whole Foods certainly contributed to that.

00:21:01.099 --> 00:21:02.889
And they contributed to our success.

00:21:02.909 --> 00:21:05.909
We wouldn't be what we are today without growth of our

00:21:04.619 --> 00:21:05.619
suppliers.

00:21:05.619 --> 00:21:09.119
I don't know whether you're aware of this or not, but there'

00:21:08.779 --> 00:21:12.379
s a complete renascence going on about local agriculture in

00:21:12.399 --> 00:21:13.809
the United States.

00:21:13.829 --> 00:21:15.269
And it's very exciting.

00:21:15.289 --> 00:21:17.289
Whole Foods is trying to contribute to that.

00:21:17.029 --> 00:21:20.279
We've made -- We have a local loan program, we've made, we'

00:21:19.230 --> 00:21:22.440
ve loaned out three million dollars to small producers

00:21:22.460 --> 00:21:27.240
around the United States, Canada and the UK.

00:21:27.259 --> 00:21:30.849
We're also trying to work with small, poor farmers around

00:21:30.869 --> 00:21:31.869
the world.

00:21:31.869 --> 00:21:35.720
Our whole trade guarantee, we've done over 200 million

00:21:35.740 --> 00:21:38.579
dollars in 2009 in whole trade goods.

00:21:38.599 --> 00:21:43.359
We work with a variety of partners, this is a good thing.

00:21:43.379 --> 00:21:47.619
So I do think that business has a responsibility to be, to

00:21:47.639 --> 00:21:50.149
be responsible for the communities and the environment.

00:21:50.169 --> 00:21:53.680
And I think that citizenship is the appropriate metaphor.

00:21:53.700 --> 00:21:56.569
That business should be good citizens in whatever

00:21:56.589 --> 00:21:58.859
communities they do business in.

00:21:58.879 --> 00:22:02.069
Good citizens in their local communities, their national

00:22:02.089 --> 00:22:04.379
communities and the global communities.

00:22:04.399 --> 00:22:08.149
I think philanthropy is consistent with citizenship and I

00:22:08.169 --> 00:22:10.419
also believe that philanthropy is not theft from the

00:22:10.329 --> 00:22:11.329
shareholders.

00:22:11.329 --> 00:22:14.579
It needs to be managed, just as you manage any other part of

00:22:13.990 --> 00:22:16.149
the business, it needs to be done intelligently.

00:22:16.169 --> 00:22:19.950
But if done intelligently, it creates a win-win-win strategy

00:22:19.970 --> 00:22:22.470
that creates value for all of the stakeholders including the

00:22:22.299 --> 00:22:23.299
shareholders.

00:22:23.299 --> 00:22:28.349
In Whole Foods case, we donate a minimum of 5% of our

00:22:28.369 --> 00:22:30.129
profits to not for profit organizations.

00:22:30.149 --> 00:22:33.069
90% of that is done locally in our stores.

00:22:33.089 --> 00:22:35.399
We support literally thousands and thousands of not for

00:22:35.419 --> 00:22:36.849
profit organizations each year.

00:22:36.869 --> 00:22:39.329
We also have a foundation called the whole planet

00:22:39.349 --> 00:22:41.230
foundation.

00:22:41.250 --> 00:22:44.250
This is commitment of our global citizenship.

00:22:44.269 --> 00:22:47.149
We work with Muhammad Yunus and the Grameem bank to make

00:22:47.169 --> 00:22:48.849
these micro-credit loans.

00:22:48.869 --> 00:22:51.879
We've --these are the countries we currently work in.

00:22:51.899 --> 00:22:55.899
These are communities that we are actually trading in, so we

00:22:55.919 --> 00:22:57.419
don't just do these randomly.

00:22:57.439 --> 00:23:00.069
It's wherever our business is trading in the developing

00:23:00.089 --> 00:23:04.480
world, we look to eventually set-up a micro-credit

00:23:04.500 --> 00:23:05.500
operation.

00:23:05.500 --> 00:23:10.399
98% of our loans have been to women, 98% repayment, no

00:23:10.419 --> 00:23:12.809
collateral, no contracts.

00:23:12.829 --> 00:23:18.129
We've now made over 57,000 loans, over 10 million dollars,

00:23:18.149 --> 00:23:21.619
and we just -- I underline Haiti because we just doubled our

00:23:21.639 --> 00:23:25.569
commitment to Haiti as a result of the earthquake.

00:23:25.589 --> 00:23:29.089
So we're up to a million dollars now in Haiti in our micro-

00:23:28.289 --> 00:23:30.730
credit loans.

00:23:30.750 --> 00:23:33.129
Eventually we're gonna cover the planet.

00:23:33.149 --> 00:23:35.710
This program, this is an example of win-win philanthropy,

00:23:35.730 --> 00:23:37.779
win-win-win philanthropy.

00:23:37.799 --> 00:23:41.049
There's no single thing our company has ever done in our 30

00:23:40.339 --> 00:23:43.589
year history that has created better team member moral than

00:23:43.609 --> 00:23:45.190
this.

00:23:45.210 --> 00:23:48.919
So for a mere 10 million dollars, we've received hundreds of

00:23:48.939 --> 00:23:52.089
millions of dollars of good will with our team member base

00:23:52.109 --> 00:23:53.690
and with our customers.

00:23:53.710 --> 00:23:56.210
We've engaged our suppliers in what we call supplier

00:23:56.159 --> 00:24:01.329
alliances, so that they're also investing in the -- in the

00:24:01.349 --> 00:24:02.349
whole planet foundation.

00:24:02.349 --> 00:24:04.779
Which, we always give our customers opportunities to make

00:24:04.799 --> 00:24:07.069
contributions.

00:24:07.089 --> 00:24:09.019
Whole Foods cares deeply about our environment.

00:24:09.039 --> 00:24:12.240
We support local, organic and sustainable agriculture.

00:24:12.259 --> 00:24:15.419
Sustainable seafood is one of the environmental issues you

00:24:15.439 --> 00:24:18.439
don't hear much about, which is unfortunate, it's a serious

00:24:17.189 --> 00:24:18.189
problem.

00:24:18.189 --> 00:24:23.409
We've worked with Mary Stewardship Council, we have farmed

00:24:23.429 --> 00:24:26.169
fish standards, and we're striving towards complete

00:24:26.189 --> 00:24:28.559
transparency on our sustainability.

00:24:28.579 --> 00:24:31.970
Whole Foods is one of the largest re -- companies in the, in

00:24:31.990 --> 00:24:33.859
the world.

00:24:33.879 --> 00:24:36.019
Committed to alternative energy, committed to green

00:24:36.039 --> 00:24:37.089
building.

00:24:37.109 --> 00:24:40.190
We have store green mission teams.

00:24:40.210 --> 00:24:41.539
Committed to animal welfare standards.

00:24:41.559 --> 00:24:45.569
Again, environmental strategy should be thought of as a win-

00:24:45.589 --> 00:24:50.480
win-win strategy that benefits all of the stakeholders.

00:24:50.500 --> 00:24:53.599
We're launching this year in 2010 our 5 step animal welfare

00:24:53.619 --> 00:24:54.809
rating program.

00:24:54.829 --> 00:24:56.349
Look for that in our stores.

00:24:56.369 --> 00:24:59.119
We've created a fo -- originally this was a foundation we

00:24:58.720 --> 00:24:59.720
started ourselves.

00:24:59.720 --> 00:25:02.639
It's called the animal compassion foundation.

00:25:02.659 --> 00:25:05.409
We've decided, we've worked with the animal welfare groups

00:25:04.779 --> 00:25:08.369
like humane society, PETA, animal welfare international.

00:25:08.389 --> 00:25:11.139
We decided to take the -- make this a public foundation and

00:25:11.159 --> 00:25:12.159
globalize it.

00:25:12.159 --> 00:25:14.909
We want our 5 step animal welfare system, we're gonna,

00:25:14.740 --> 00:25:17.169
whether than monopolize those standards, we're giving those

00:25:17.189 --> 00:25:18.439
standards that we've created.

00:25:18.389 --> 00:25:21.389
We've worked on it for five years to create our standards,

00:25:21.139 --> 00:25:22.389
we're giving those up.

00:25:22.139 --> 00:25:24.859
Hopefully for everybody else to begin to use.

00:25:24.879 --> 00:25:25.879
[Pause]

00:25:24.879 --> 00:25:31.879
John: More time, I'd go into more detail about this system,

00:25:31.899 --> 00:25:34.269
no time.

00:25:34.289 --> 00:25:37.779
Conscious capitalism and not for profit organizations, what

00:25:37.799 --> 00:25:40.309
does this have to do with not for profit organizations?

00:25:40.329 --> 00:25:44.980
Not for profits get one of the most important aspects of

00:25:45.000 --> 00:25:46.059
conscious business.

00:25:46.079 --> 00:25:50.049
They understand the importance of having a higher purpose

00:25:50.069 --> 00:25:51.069
and having a mission.

00:25:51.069 --> 00:25:54.069
That's their gift that they had to teach the for profit

00:25:53.970 --> 00:25:55.609
section.

00:25:55.629 --> 00:25:59.139
However, non-profits are not able to transcend self

00:25:59.159 --> 00:26:00.159
interest.

00:26:00.159 --> 00:26:03.220
You will not become and angel just because you work for a

00:26:03.240 --> 00:26:06.490
non-profit, or work for the government.

00:26:06.509 --> 00:26:09.929
Often times there's the anti=profit mentality that severely

00:26:09.949 --> 00:26:12.710
cri -- cripples, creates inefficiencies, wastes and

00:26:12.730 --> 00:26:14.099
stagnation.

00:26:14.119 --> 00:26:16.279
Therefore many not for profit organizations are not

00:26:16.299 --> 00:26:19.190
sustainable over the long run.

00:26:19.210 --> 00:26:24.129
They need to learn from corporations about surviving and

00:26:24.149 --> 00:26:25.919
flourishing.

00:26:25.939 --> 00:26:28.939
So I argue they need to also evolve to a more holistic

00:26:28.730 --> 00:26:29.859
model.

00:26:29.879 --> 00:26:34.589
This is the model of a conscious non-profit organization,

00:26:34.609 --> 00:26:35.669
does it look familiar?

00:26:35.689 --> 00:26:36.689
[Audience Mumbles]

00:26:35.689 --> 00:26:40.859
John: It should, it looks -- it's exactly the same as the

00:26:40.879 --> 00:26:44.879
conscious enterprise model that I showed with Whole Foods.

00:26:44.899 --> 00:26:48.730
The only difference is that -- at the core you've got a

00:26:48.750 --> 00:26:52.509
social mission as opposed to necessarily a business mission.

00:26:52.529 --> 00:26:56.399
And instead of investors, you have donors.

00:26:56.419 --> 00:26:59.539
Otherwise, it's pretty much the same model.

00:26:59.559 --> 00:27:00.559
[Pause]

00:26:59.559 --> 00:27:10.480
John: This illustrates to me the flaw in the thinking that

00:27:10.500 --> 00:27:14.440
many people have about non-profits, government and

00:27:14.460 --> 00:27:15.460
corporations.

00:27:15.460 --> 00:27:19.929
We believe that because non-profits have a higher purpose

00:27:19.949 --> 00:27:25.329
that they are good, they're altruistic, they do all types of

00:27:25.349 --> 00:27:26.349
wonderful things.

00:27:26.349 --> 00:27:33.099
On the other side of the wall, we have corporations.

00:27:33.119 --> 00:27:36.659
And, they are greedy, they are selfish, they only care about

00:27:36.679 --> 00:27:37.679
profit.

00:27:37.679 --> 00:27:44.409
I want to tear this wall down, this way of thinking is not

00:27:44.429 --> 00:27:47.750
gonna serve humanity in the 21st century, we've gotta tear

00:27:47.769 --> 00:27:51.419
this wall down.

00:27:51.439 --> 00:27:56.589
This exaggerated polarity and dualism between egoism and

00:27:56.609 --> 00:27:58.799
altruism is false.

00:27:58.819 --> 00:28:03.519
Human beings are both self-interested and we're capable of

00:28:03.539 --> 00:28:06.980
love.

00:28:07.000 --> 00:28:10.500
We don't need a win-lose metaphor or a lose-win metaphor, we

00:28:10.039 --> 00:28:12.529
need win-win-win metaphors.

00:28:12.549 --> 00:28:17.200
Creative love, love of self and love of others is the golden

00:28:17.220 --> 00:28:20.429
Aristotelian mean that breaks down the dichotomy between

00:28:20.449 --> 00:28:23.059
egoism and altruism.

00:28:23.079 --> 00:28:27.409
You can see that the upward spiral of caring starts with

00:28:27.429 --> 00:28:31.399
your self, moves to you family, then to your friends, then

00:28:31.419 --> 00:28:35.059
to your tribe, or to you community, or your company,

00:28:35.079 --> 00:28:38.529
potentially moving to your race or your nation, potentially

00:28:38.549 --> 00:28:41.519
then expanding onto humanity as a whole.

00:28:41.539 --> 00:28:44.480
Possibly your ability to empathize can extend to all

00:28:44.500 --> 00:28:48.569
mammals, perhaps even to all sentient beings and perhaps

00:28:48.589 --> 00:28:51.659
even to all life.

00:28:51.679 --> 00:28:58.589
That is the potential that humanities have, humanity have.

00:28:58.609 --> 00:29:03.359
Conscious business is not the same thing as CSR.

00:29:03.379 --> 00:29:07.869
They're different.

00:29:07.889 --> 00:29:10.700
On the left, you see corporate-social responsibility.

00:29:10.720 --> 00:29:11.869
It's very consistent.

00:29:11.889 --> 00:29:16.189
CSR's very consistent with the traditional business model.

00:29:16.209 --> 00:29:18.849
It's consistent because shareholders still rule instead of

00:29:18.869 --> 00:29:19.869
stakeholders.

00:29:19.869 --> 00:29:23.230
It has nothing to do with the corporate purpose.

00:29:23.250 --> 00:29:27.399
It still has a traditional mechanistic view of business.

00:29:27.419 --> 00:29:30.559
It's usually an add-on, just an add-on.

00:29:30.579 --> 00:29:32.079
Something they graft onto the business.

00:29:32.099 --> 00:29:35.349
Ah were getting a lot of stuff about this CSR stuff, we can

00:29:35.349 --> 00:29:36.889
get some good publicity.

00:29:36.909 --> 00:29:40.119
Let's create a CSR department/

00:29:40.139 --> 00:29:42.710
Often times it reports up through the public relations

00:29:42.730 --> 00:29:44.710
department.

00:29:44.730 --> 00:29:48.899
So, it's -- when business and corporations get accused of

00:29:48.919 --> 00:29:52.960
brain-washing, often times the accusations correct.

00:29:52.980 --> 00:29:54.980
It is a simple for of brain-washing.

00:29:55.000 --> 00:29:59.779
Whereas the conscious business -- the social mission, the

00:29:59.799 --> 00:30:02.659
environmental mission are at the very essence, the very

00:30:02.679 --> 00:30:05.679
core, at the heart of why the business exists in the first

00:30:05.379 --> 00:30:06.379
place.

00:30:06.379 --> 00:30:11.210
It's not a grafting on, instead it's at the purpose, the

00:30:11.230 --> 00:30:12.829
center.

00:30:12.849 --> 00:30:19.809
You can meet the CSR through, through a charitable gesture

00:30:19.829 --> 00:30:21.049
can meet that.

00:30:21.069 --> 00:30:24.129
But the conscious business requires a genuine

00:30:24.149 --> 00:30:26.329
transformation.

00:30:26.349 --> 00:30:30.089
The CSR assumes that all good deeds are equally desirable,

00:30:30.109 --> 00:30:33.759
but the conscious business, the good deeds should correlate

00:30:33.779 --> 00:30:37.109
back to advancing the company's core mission.

00:30:37.129 --> 00:30:42.289
Here's what I wanna see happen in the 21st, that we will

00:30:42.309 --> 00:30:44.009
evolve from this to this.

00:30:44.029 --> 00:30:46.589
From profit focus to purpose focus.

00:30:46.609 --> 00:30:48.629
From only shareholding -- shareholders winning to all

00:30:48.649 --> 00:30:49.649
stakeholders winning.

00:30:49.649 --> 00:30:51.179
From short term to long term.

00:30:51.199 --> 00:30:54.470
From zero sum to win-win-win strategies.

00:30:54.490 --> 00:30:55.929
From self-centered to holistic.

00:30:55.949 --> 00:30:59.629
From conflicts of interest to synergies or to harmony of

00:30:59.649 --> 00:31:00.649
interest.

00:31:00.649 --> 00:31:02.339
Parasitic to mutualistic

00:31:02.359 --> 00:31:04.549
From exploitative to creating value.

00:31:04.569 --> 00:31:06.240
From trade-off to synergies.

00:31:06.259 --> 00:31:08.710
From disliked to valued and loved.

00:31:08.730 --> 00:31:14.169
And from not trusted to trusted.

00:31:14.189 --> 00:31:17.119
Conscious business and conscious capitalism are new

00:31:17.139 --> 00:31:20.349
paradigms for the 21st century.

00:31:20.369 --> 00:31:24.019
To just sum it up, you have to support capitalism, the

00:31:24.039 --> 00:31:28.230
conscious capitalist still supports they key economic rights

00:31:28.250 --> 00:31:29.609
that underlie all capitalism.

00:31:29.629 --> 00:31:32.589
Property right, freedom of contract, freedom to trade and

00:31:32.609 --> 00:31:34.710
the rule of law.

00:31:34.730 --> 00:31:37.349
The conscious business goes further, identifies a deeper

00:31:37.369 --> 00:31:40.629
purpose beyond maximizing profit and shareholder value.

00:31:40.649 --> 00:31:44.000
The enterprise is then managed in such a way to create value

00:31:44.019 --> 00:31:46.730
simultaneously for all of the inter-dependant stakeholders.

00:31:46.750 --> 00:31:51.490
The leadership becomes conscious to the enterprise and

00:31:51.509 --> 00:31:54.500
serves its mission and its stakeholders.

00:31:54.519 --> 00:31:58.500
We transform the underlying ethics of the enterprise to a

00:31:58.519 --> 00:32:01.769
win-win-win-win philosophy.

00:32:01.789 --> 00:32:07.859
This allows, finally for business and capitalism to do what

00:32:07.879 --> 00:32:10.609
human beings want it to do.

00:32:10.629 --> 00:32:15.079
Which is to aspire to fulfill the deepest aspirations that

00:32:15.099 --> 00:32:16.609
humanity has.

00:32:16.629 --> 00:32:19.220
The good, the true, the beautiful and the heroic.

00:32:19.240 --> 00:32:21.539
thank you very much.

00:32:21.559 --> 00:32:22.559
[Applause]

00:32:21.559 --> 00:32:38.519
John: Alright, I did save a little time for questions.

00:32:38.539 --> 00:32:41.039
I did rapidly go through a lot of that stuff.

00:32:41.039 --> 00:32:43.129
So, how are we doing the questions?

00:32:43.149 --> 00:32:46.149
Mike, am I gonna call on people, are the mikes being passed

00:32:45.779 --> 00:32:46.779
around?

00:32:46.779 --> 00:32:47.779
&gt;&gt;I've got it.

00:32:47.529 --> 00:32:50.029
John: You got the mikes, great, so just fire away.

00:32:50.029 --> 00:32:51.529
&gt;&gt;Great, thank you so much John.

00:32:51.529 --> 00:32:56.359
As we are mostly focused on start-up strategies, we get a

00:32:56.379 --> 00:32:58.379
lot of founders and entrepreneurs that come in.

00:32:58.129 --> 00:32:59.129
John: Mmm Hmm

00:32:59.129 --> 00:33:01.879
&gt;&gt;And a lot of times they speak of finding a company,

00:33:01.879 --> 00:33:04.879
running it until it gets to a certain size, and then the

00:33:04.879 --> 00:33:07.629
investors aboard decide that it'd be better to have someone

00:33:07.379 --> 00:33:09.129
perhaps with industry experience to run it.

00:33:09.129 --> 00:33:10.879
And that your store is very different.

00:33:10.879 --> 00:33:13.629
And so, I guess from a perspective of a leadership question,

00:33:13.629 --> 00:33:15.879
what were specific leadership challenges that you had while

00:33:15.879 --> 00:33:18.829
you company grew underneath you, and you went from being a

00:33:18.849 --> 00:33:21.849
founder to a small business owner to you know the CEO of

00:33:21.349 --> 00:33:22.349
Whole Foods?

00:33:22.349 --> 00:33:26.779
John: Weeellll, there's 30 years of history there, so.

00:33:26.799 --> 00:33:30.049
I will say that if you start your own business, I came up

00:33:29.919 --> 00:33:31.889
with a metaphor for venture capitalists.

00:33:31.909 --> 00:33:35.659
They don't like it too much, but I think it's a pretty good

00:33:34.289 --> 00:33:35.289
metaphor.

00:33:35.289 --> 00:33:36.970
They're like hitchhikers.

00:33:36.990 --> 00:33:38.490
They're hitchhikers with credit cards

00:33:38.189 --> 00:33:39.189
[Audience Laughs]

00:33:38.189 --> 00:33:43.409
John: And as long as you get them to where they want to go,

00:33:43.429 --> 00:33:45.980
they will help pay for the gas.

00:33:46.000 --> 00:33:51.039
However, if you get off track, they hijack the car, they

00:33:51.059 --> 00:33:54.559
hire a new driver and they throw you out to the side of the

00:33:53.699 --> 00:33:54.699
road.

00:33:54.699 --> 00:33:55.699
[Audience Laughter]

00:33:54.699 --> 00:33:57.199
John: We never gave the venture capitalists control of Whole

00:33:57.149 --> 00:33:58.149
Foods.

00:33:58.149 --> 00:34:00.899
We went public probably a little earlier than we would have

00:34:00.869 --> 00:34:04.339
ordinarily gone cuz we needed to raise more capital.

00:34:04.359 --> 00:34:07.359
If we'd done another round, the VC's would have gotten

00:34:06.179 --> 00:34:07.819
control of the company.

00:34:07.839 --> 00:34:12.409
So, we went ahead and "fire ready aim" became public.

00:34:12.429 --> 00:34:15.190
Probably you couldn't even become public as small as Whole

00:34:15.210 --> 00:34:20.049
Foods was back in 1982, wouldn't be possible today.

00:34:20.069 --> 00:34:26.179
In terms of the challenges, there's so many of them, I

00:34:26.199 --> 00:34:27.949
wouldn't even know where to begin.

00:34:27.699 --> 00:34:30.449
I will say though that, in essence to your question, you

00:34:30.409 --> 00:34:34.949
have to grow as a human being and as a leader or you hold

00:34:34.969 --> 00:34:37.380
your business back.

00:34:37.400 --> 00:34:41.369
I've been forced to grow, reluctantly, many times.

00:34:41.389 --> 00:34:46.369
Because if I hadn't the company wasn't reach its full

00:34:46.389 --> 00:34:47.389
potential.

00:34:47.389 --> 00:34:53.329
So, it's essential that you continue to get not only

00:34:53.349 --> 00:34:56.480
smarter, but develop your emotional intelligence.

00:34:56.500 --> 00:34:59.309
I would say for leadership, emotional intelligence is more

00:34:59.329 --> 00:35:00.719
important than I.Q.

00:35:00.739 --> 00:35:04.630
And I would argue that social intelligence, and I would also

00:35:04.650 --> 00:35:05.650
argue spiritual intelligence.

00:35:05.670 --> 00:35:08.849
Spiritual intelligence being the ability to understand

00:35:08.869 --> 00:35:11.679
purpose and meaning and to be able to explain that and

00:35:11.699 --> 00:35:16.049
translate it are essential intelligences that the

00:35:16.069 --> 00:35:17.920
entrepreneurs of the 21st century need.

00:35:17.940 --> 00:35:20.690
So you're gonna face many challenges as you grow your

00:35:20.150 --> 00:35:21.150
business.

00:35:21.150 --> 00:35:23.059
First challenges is of course is money.

00:35:23.079 --> 00:35:25.329
Gotta have -- get the money, gotta have the capital.

00:35:24.909 --> 00:35:26.659
So you gotta deal with cash flow.

00:35:26.659 --> 00:35:29.849
Then as you grow, you have to be able to create structures

00:35:29.869 --> 00:35:32.579
that simultaneously allow the business to fulfill its

00:35:32.599 --> 00:35:36.380
potential, but without hamstringing it.

00:35:36.400 --> 00:35:42.000
So they have to be flexible structures that can evolve.

00:35:42.019 --> 00:35:45.769
I mean, if we had --if we sat down one on one I could go

00:35:44.670 --> 00:35:45.670
over in some detail.

00:35:45.670 --> 00:35:48.329
But there are different stages that a business goes through,

00:35:48.349 --> 00:35:51.389
and different crisis that a business meets.

00:35:51.409 --> 00:35:54.659
And, just like as you go through your own life cycle, you'll

00:35:54.309 --> 00:35:55.309
have different crisis.

00:35:55.309 --> 00:36:00.199
In your early 20's it's like finding a partner and finding

00:36:00.219 --> 00:36:02.670
work, that's your crisis.

00:36:02.690 --> 00:36:05.440
When you get in your 30's you're raising kids.

00:36:04.940 --> 00:36:08.690
When you get into you know 40's and 50's, it starts to be

00:36:08.110 --> 00:36:11.319
about generativeness and what your legacy's gonna be.

00:36:11.339 --> 00:36:13.739
That's all I've gotten so far so,

00:36:13.759 --> 00:36:14.759
[Laughter]

00:36:13.759 --> 00:36:17.259
John: When I get a little older I'll find out what the next

00:36:16.980 --> 00:36:17.980
crisis is.

00:36:17.980 --> 00:36:18.980
Probably body falling apart.

00:36:18.980 --> 00:36:19.980
[Laughter]

00:36:18.980 --> 00:36:22.230
John: Despite Whole Foods, it hasn't -- I don't think it's

00:36:21.469 --> 00:36:22.549
made me immortal.

00:36:22.569 --> 00:36:24.750
So, I'll deal with my own problems.

00:36:24.769 --> 00:36:25.980
Next Question.

00:36:26.000 --> 00:36:30.130
&gt;&gt; So, the Supreme Court recently came down on the side of

00:36:30.150 --> 00:36:33.809
effectively letting corporations play a more active role in

00:36:33.829 --> 00:36:35.549
public political discourse.

00:36:35.569 --> 00:36:38.549
And the liberal wing of the court --I'm over here.

00:36:38.569 --> 00:36:39.569
John: Where are you?

00:36:39.569 --> 00:36:40.569
There you are.

00:36:40.319 --> 00:36:44.699
&gt;&gt; So the, the liberal wing of the court made a distinction

00:36:44.719 --> 00:36:47.670
between corporate speak and individual speak.

00:36:47.690 --> 00:36:50.940
And I was wondering where you come down on the side of that

00:36:49.639 --> 00:36:50.639
debate?

00:36:50.639 --> 00:36:53.969
Both in terms of Whole Foods and kind of the prevailing

00:36:53.989 --> 00:36:59.719
business atmosphere in terms of, excuse me, whether -- that

00:36:59.739 --> 00:37:02.759
new opportunity may be used more opportunistically by other

00:37:02.779 --> 00:37:04.529
than it might be by Whole Foods.

00:37:03.779 --> 00:37:04.779
John:

00:37:04.779 --> 00:37:08.710
[Sigh] You know every time I ever take on a political

00:37:08.730 --> 00:37:12.929
question, I get totally slammed in the media.

00:37:12.949 --> 00:37:17.289
So, on the other hand, I aspire to authenticity, so what am

00:37:17.309 --> 00:37:18.309
I supposed to do.?

00:37:18.309 --> 00:37:19.309
[Laughter]

00:37:18.309 --> 00:37:19.309
[Applause]

00:37:18.309 --> 00:37:27.509
John: Let me try to be cagey on this one, and authentic.

00:37:27.529 --> 00:37:30.889
I don't believe that media corporations such as the New York

00:37:30.909 --> 00:37:34.719
Times and the Wall Street Journal and Forbes, that they

00:37:34.739 --> 00:37:37.819
should have the right for freedom of speech because they own

00:37:37.839 --> 00:37:39.409
media assets.

00:37:39.429 --> 00:37:43.639
And other corporations therefore don't because they don?t

00:37:43.659 --> 00:37:44.659
own media assets.

00:37:44.659 --> 00:37:48.179
So, as a general rule, I don't think corporations are eager

00:37:48.199 --> 00:37:51.179
to -- you pay a price when you speak out publically.

00:37:51.199 --> 00:37:53.199
Trust me, I found that out this summer.

00:37:53.199 --> 00:37:57.339
So, I don't think this ruling, I do think it's consistent

00:37:57.359 --> 00:37:58.559
with freedom of speech.

00:37:58.579 --> 00:38:02.429
And I, I, I was happy with the ruling.

00:38:02.449 --> 00:38:06.969
So, mostly because I, I don't think it's gonna be all the

00:38:06.989 --> 00:38:09.239
negative things that people believes gonna happened is gonna

00:38:08.739 --> 00:38:09.739
happen.

00:38:09.739 --> 00:38:11.119
But time will tell.

00:38:11.139 --> 00:38:12.929
Next Question.

00:38:12.949 --> 00:38:15.880
&gt;&gt;So you might have to be cagey again here.

00:38:15.900 --> 00:38:18.719
John: I don't want to talk about climate change, or

00:38:18.739 --> 00:38:19.739
healthcare reform.

00:38:19.739 --> 00:38:20.739
[Laughter]

00:38:19.739 --> 00:38:23.239
&gt;&gt;OK.

00:38:23.259 --> 00:38:26.289
So, I don't necessarily agree with this argument, but I want

00:38:26.309 --> 00:38:28.309
to see how you respond to the argument.

00:38:28.309 --> 00:38:31.449
Why not simply have the government enforce the conscious

00:38:31.469 --> 00:38:34.230
capitalism that you're advocating for.

00:38:34.250 --> 00:38:38.239
Whether that be, you know, subsidizing green project, or

00:38:38.259 --> 00:38:39.509
healthcare reform or what not.

00:38:39.509 --> 00:38:46.429
John: Well cuz I might be wrong.

00:38:46.449 --> 00:38:47.449
[Sigh] I mean

00:38:47.449 --> 00:38:48.449
[Pause]

00:38:47.449 --> 00:38:55.179
John: People believe that if they had ultimate power, they

00:38:55.199 --> 00:38:56.380
would be good.

00:38:56.400 --> 00:39:01.049
And, I don't believe in a society where government is

00:39:01.069 --> 00:39:03.690
legislating a particular vision on the way people should

00:39:03.710 --> 00:39:04.710
live.

00:39:04.710 --> 00:39:07.259
Even if I happen to be my vision.

00:39:07.279 --> 00:39:10.750
I believe in a pluralistic society where we get to

00:39:10.769 --> 00:39:12.400
experiment.

00:39:12.420 --> 00:39:15.920
And that one of the secrets to Whole Foods is that we're a

00:39:15.159 --> 00:39:16.159
very experimental company.

00:39:16.159 --> 00:39:18.279
We try thing, most of them fail.

00:39:18.299 --> 00:39:21.299
But we throw out the failures and we continue to learn and

00:39:20.599 --> 00:39:21.599
evolve.

00:39:21.599 --> 00:39:26.769
So I believe in a society that's democratic and allows -- I

00:39:26.789 --> 00:39:29.779
believe conscious capitalism by the way is going to triumph

00:39:29.799 --> 00:39:31.549
at the end of the 21st century.

00:39:30.880 --> 00:39:31.880
Why?

00:39:31.880 --> 00:39:34.420
Not because government has to pass these laws to force

00:39:34.440 --> 00:39:35.690
people to be that way.

00:39:35.690 --> 00:39:37.039
Because it works better.

00:39:37.059 --> 00:39:40.309
It's gonna win in the market place, it's gonna win in

00:39:39.179 --> 00:39:40.179
competition.

00:39:40.179 --> 00:39:43.429
So these ideas need to be tried out and if they work, they'

00:39:43.380 --> 00:39:45.119
ll flourish and expand.

00:39:45.139 --> 00:39:48.639
If they don't work, they'll be thrown in the trash bin of

00:39:47.809 --> 00:39:48.809
history.

00:39:48.809 --> 00:39:52.210
And, having government pick winners for business

00:39:52.230 --> 00:39:54.079
philosophies is a very bad idea.

00:39:54.099 --> 00:39:57.349
First of all cuz they probably won't pick the ideas that you

00:39:56.849 --> 00:39:57.849
agree with anyway.

00:39:57.849 --> 00:40:01.099
So, I don't want to live in a society where government is

00:40:00.949 --> 00:40:02.980
telling us how businesses should operate.

00:40:03.000 --> 00:40:05.000
I think that the marketplace should determine that.

00:40:05.000 --> 00:40:06.000
[Pause]

00:40:05.000 --> 00:40:11.259
&gt;&gt;Hi, can you talk about some of the difficult decisions

00:40:11.279 --> 00:40:14.460
you've had to make with your supply chain when you -- as

00:40:14.480 --> 00:40:17.259
Whole Foods has grown over the past twenty years.

00:40:17.279 --> 00:40:20.029
John: Could you give me a little more color on that?

00:40:19.779 --> 00:40:20.779
I mean what?

00:40:20.779 --> 00:40:23.279
&gt;&gt; In terms of maintaining the integrity of what you wanted

00:40:23.279 --> 00:40:26.279
in a supplier when you first started a business by what you

00:40:26.279 --> 00:40:29.269
can get as you scaled to be such a large company.

00:40:29.289 --> 00:40:32.449
John: The first crisis of faith of Whole Foods was when we

00:40:32.469 --> 00:40:38.049
realized our suppliers were mostly -- weren't -- they, they

00:40:38.069 --> 00:40:40.460
weren't as good as their marketing materials indicated.

00:40:40.480 --> 00:40:45.380
Some disillusion of a retailer because as we got in there

00:40:45.400 --> 00:40:48.900
and looked beneath the Kimono so to speak, a lot of what we

00:40:48.920 --> 00:40:52.119
saw was not that pretty.

00:40:52.139 --> 00:40:54.219
For example, in the meat industry.

00:40:54.239 --> 00:40:57.239
If you really knew what they were doing to those animals out

00:40:57.250 --> 00:41:00.219
there, you'd probably all become vegetarians or vegans.

00:41:00.239 --> 00:41:01.239
It's terrible.

00:41:01.239 --> 00:41:04.489
And as we've gotten into the process, and we've seen more.

00:41:04.269 --> 00:41:09.079
So that's the first challenge, is the retailers, the middle

00:41:09.099 --> 00:41:12.219
man right between the farmer, the rancher, the manufacturer

00:41:12.239 --> 00:41:13.750
and the consumer.

00:41:13.769 --> 00:41:21.139
And the consumer trust us to, to deliver what the

00:41:21.159 --> 00:41:22.250
manufacturer claims.

00:41:22.269 --> 00:41:24.429
They blame us if it's not.

00:41:24.449 --> 00:41:26.710
So that was the first challenge that we had.

00:41:26.730 --> 00:41:28.460
And it's an ongoing challenge.

00:41:28.480 --> 00:41:31.949
We have to look very deeply at our supply chain and

00:41:31.969 --> 00:41:37.500
determine whether or not it's what it's cracked up to be.

00:41:37.519 --> 00:41:40.019
And so we're doing lots of things now, like.

00:41:39.579 --> 00:41:41.529
We have our animal welfare rating system.

00:41:41.549 --> 00:41:44.549
We're gonna put all of our -- everybody we buy meat from

00:41:44.299 --> 00:41:47.690
which is thousands and thousands of people will have to get

00:41:47.710 --> 00:41:50.819
-- every -- will have to get rated in terms of the way they

00:41:50.839 --> 00:41:53.339
treat their animals, so we can give transparency to our

00:41:52.589 --> 00:41:53.589
customers.

00:41:53.589 --> 00:41:57.329
Seafood sustainability's another one that is very important.

00:41:57.349 --> 00:42:00.349
And people are not aware that so many of the species they

00:42:00.319 --> 00:42:02.319
buy in seafood departments are not sustainable.

00:42:02.339 --> 00:42:05.159
And yet, the retailers are not telling the customer that.

00:42:05.179 --> 00:42:07.929
So they're not -- They're keeping that as a secret.

00:42:07.089 --> 00:42:11.179
Se we're gonna basically be much more transparent about

00:42:11.199 --> 00:42:12.309
that.

00:42:12.329 --> 00:42:15.079
There's all kinds of -- the first organic supplier Whole

00:42:15.099 --> 00:42:16.099
Foods bought from.

00:42:16.099 --> 00:42:18.349
For example, anecdotally here was a company in Southern

00:42:18.259 --> 00:42:19.259
California.

00:42:19.259 --> 00:42:24.069
It was called sunburst farms.

00:42:24.089 --> 00:42:26.849
And, you read their marketing materials, they're organic,

00:42:26.869 --> 00:42:28.969
they're this community living there.

00:42:28.989 --> 00:42:32.239
And it looks like they're love and peace and joy and it

00:42:32.210 --> 00:42:33.739
seems like a really wonderful thing.

00:42:33.759 --> 00:42:36.759
We checked -- we got into it, we checked them out and it

00:42:36.549 --> 00:42:41.360
turns out they were like the guy that was a cult leader.

00:42:41.380 --> 00:42:44.130
And they were a survivalist group what had a whole bunch ---

00:42:43.969 --> 00:42:47.130
they were they were stocking up guns for Armageddon that was

00:42:47.150 --> 00:42:48.150
coming.

00:42:48.150 --> 00:42:51.210
And in fact, they weren't selling that much organic produce,

00:42:51.230 --> 00:42:56.449
it was mostly -- they were taking -- they had organic boxes

00:42:56.469 --> 00:42:58.969
and they were taking commercial produce and stinking it into

00:42:58.429 --> 00:43:00.179
the organic boxes and shipping it out.

00:43:00.179 --> 00:43:03.429
So there was a lot of fraud that we had to deal with.

00:43:03.429 --> 00:43:06.429
Now, we're in this enviable position that we've gotten large

00:43:05.929 --> 00:43:11.119
enough that we can go work with our suppliers in a more

00:43:11.139 --> 00:43:14.940
transparent way to assure ourselves and our customer that

00:43:14.960 --> 00:43:17.610
the people are really getting what they've been promised.

00:43:17.630 --> 00:43:20.880
So if you're smaller, it's a lot harder to do that.

00:43:20.789 --> 00:43:26.049
So, there is fraud that goes on in business I'm sorry to

00:43:26.069 --> 00:43:27.069
say.

00:43:27.069 --> 00:43:30.339
And, so that was one of our challenges we had to deal with.

00:43:30.359 --> 00:43:31.359
Next question.

00:43:31.359 --> 00:43:33.889
Sean: Hi, Sean off here to you right.

00:43:33.909 --> 00:43:34.909
John: Stand -- Go ahead.

00:43:34.909 --> 00:43:37.679
Sean: Originally my families been Whole Foods shoppers for

00:43:37.699 --> 00:43:38.699
20 years.

00:43:38.699 --> 00:43:41.949
We're big fans, and I also was a former front end team

00:43:41.239 --> 00:43:43.230
leader at you Richardson Texas store so.

00:43:43.250 --> 00:43:45.000
John: Look how far you've come.

00:43:44.750 --> 00:43:45.750
[Laughter]

00:43:44.750 --> 00:43:46.500
Sean: Made it all the way, so

00:43:46.500 --> 00:43:47.500
[Applause]

00:43:46.500 --> 00:43:49.469
Sean: Big fan, thanks for your time.

00:43:49.489 --> 00:43:52.239
Question for you, about ten to fifteen years ago, it seemed

00:43:51.549 --> 00:43:54.909
like there was a shift from -- Whole Foods started stocking

00:43:54.929 --> 00:43:56.739
Lays potato chips and conventional items.

00:43:56.759 --> 00:44:00.009
And it seemed like there might have been a shift in you know

00:43:58.799 --> 00:44:01.549
the culture or the core business segment you guys were going

00:44:00.589 --> 00:44:01.589
after.

00:44:01.589 --> 00:44:04.339
And then a few years later went back and eliminated those

00:44:04.339 --> 00:44:05.839
products and focused more on organics.

00:44:05.839 --> 00:44:08.839
Just trying to figure out what kind of the thinking was of

00:44:08.839 --> 00:44:11.589
the company at that two year period, and then kinda where

00:44:11.589 --> 00:44:12.839
you guys went to there.

00:44:12.839 --> 00:44:15.339
John: Well, you can't understand Whole Foods unless you

00:44:15.089 --> 00:44:16.839
understand how decentralized and empowered we are.

00:44:16.839 --> 00:44:19.839
That was not a decision that was made in Austin Texas for

00:44:19.759 --> 00:44:24.400
the whole company that was probably made locally in a store

00:44:24.420 --> 00:44:25.420
or a region.

00:44:25.420 --> 00:44:27.679
And it was done as an experiment.

00:44:27.699 --> 00:44:31.750
So, the experiment was taken -- went out and customers didn'

00:44:31.769 --> 00:44:34.699
t like it, and the experiment was just a failure.

00:44:34.719 --> 00:44:38.339
So that was, that was not a companywide experiment.

00:44:38.359 --> 00:44:43.059
So, again, most of our experiment, they most of them fail.

00:44:43.079 --> 00:44:45.829
But, and that was one I would argue, that, that, that

00:44:45.529 --> 00:44:46.529
failed.

00:44:46.529 --> 00:44:49.730
But it was important that the region and the store get a

00:44:49.750 --> 00:44:52.139
chance to test that idea out.

00:44:52.159 --> 00:44:54.690
It might be disillusioning, but again, most of the suppliers

00:44:54.710 --> 00:44:59.639
that were in that early days, they've mostly sold out to

00:44:59.659 --> 00:45:03.920
like Pepsi co. Frito Oley, Coca Cola Kraft, General Foods.

00:45:03.940 --> 00:45:07.009
Most of the natural food labels now are in the hands of

00:45:07.029 --> 00:45:09.529
those larger food manufacturers.

00:45:09.549 --> 00:45:13.460
And, there's less transparency there than there should be.

00:45:13.480 --> 00:45:16.230
And Whole Foods of course has got a much greater commitment

00:45:16.139 --> 00:45:18.139
to our private label now at this point.

00:45:18.139 --> 00:45:21.710
Trader Joe's is like 85% 90% private label.

00:45:21.730 --> 00:45:24.429
Whole Foods is only about 20%.

00:45:24.449 --> 00:45:29.420
So, I don't think there was any lasting cultural change, but

00:45:29.440 --> 00:45:32.630
there is continued evolution in the supply chain that we

00:45:32.650 --> 00:45:33.650
have to deal with.

00:45:33.650 --> 00:45:38.920
So I don't

00:45:38.940 --> 00:45:40.940
&gt;&gt; My question surrounds Leonard Greens lessons in Whole

00:45:40.929 --> 00:45:43.929
Foods in 2008 and how you came to the decision to take

00:45:43.949 --> 00:45:45.380
private equity capital.

00:45:45.400 --> 00:45:48.639
And how that partnership has helped your business to evolve

00:45:48.659 --> 00:45:49.659
over the last year.

00:45:49.659 --> 00:45:51.049
John: Good question

00:45:51.069 --> 00:45:52.069
[Pause]

00:45:51.069 --> 00:45:57.400
John: We bought -- The Wild Oats merger has worked out

00:45:57.420 --> 00:46:03.309
extremely well for us, but the timing of it was terrible.

00:46:03.329 --> 00:46:06.400
When we -- Of course than we got into the whole FTC thing

00:46:06.420 --> 00:46:09.670
and what a hassle that was and how much money we wasted on

00:46:08.920 --> 00:46:09.920
that.

00:46:09.920 --> 00:46:10.920
But another story.

00:46:10.670 --> 00:46:14.759
But, when we bought Wild Oats, the market -- we were kind of

00:46:14.779 --> 00:46:16.029
at our peak stock price.

00:46:16.029 --> 00:46:17.279
We paid cash for them.

00:46:17.279 --> 00:46:20.029
In order to do that, we had to go borrow money.

00:46:20.029 --> 00:46:22.029
So we took on a lot of debt.

00:46:22.029 --> 00:46:25.029
More debt than we'd ever taken on our company's history.

00:46:24.529 --> 00:46:27.779
But we didn't think it would be a big deal because we

00:46:27.529 --> 00:46:30.949
thought we'd sell stock in a couple of months and pay off,

00:46:30.969 --> 00:46:31.969
pay down the debt.

00:46:31.969 --> 00:46:34.469
But then our, the economy started going into the tank.

00:46:34.449 --> 00:46:37.609
But first of all, the merger got put off many, many months

00:46:37.629 --> 00:46:39.879
while it had to go through the regulatory processes.

00:46:39.879 --> 00:46:44.630
Then our stocks started to decline because the economy was

00:46:44.650 --> 00:46:47.400
declining, and our sales were declining, so we didn't wanna

00:46:47.319 --> 00:46:48.319
sell stock.

00:46:48.319 --> 00:46:53.190
And then, what September 2008, you can remember that time,

00:46:53.210 --> 00:46:55.230
I've never, that was a panic.

00:46:55.250 --> 00:46:58.630
That's the only panic I'm old enough to have lived through,

00:46:58.650 --> 00:47:01.150
and I'm probably the oldest guy in the room.

00:47:00.900 --> 00:47:05.569
So, it's a -- everything went to hell.

00:47:05.589 --> 00:47:10.099
And our sales started plummeting, our stock went down to $7.

00:47:10.119 --> 00:47:11.119
00.

00:47:11.119 --> 00:47:14.329
We were trading at two times our cash flow.

00:47:14.349 --> 00:47:19.299
Could have bought Whole Foods and you got a 15% return on

00:47:19.319 --> 00:47:20.859
cash flow alone.

00:47:20.879 --> 00:47:24.230
Us begging, please let me buy back stock, please let me buy

00:47:24.250 --> 00:47:25.250
back stock.

00:47:25.250 --> 00:47:27.329
But the board in their infinite wisdom

00:47:27.349 --> 00:47:28.349
[Laughter]

00:47:27.349 --> 00:47:30.969
John: Realized that what if it's not the bottom.

00:47:30.989 --> 00:47:34.009
What if it gets worse?

00:47:34.029 --> 00:47:38.150
So, that was good point of view and we took the money in

00:47:38.170 --> 00:47:39.920
from Leonard Green as an insurance policy.

00:47:39.920 --> 00:47:43.469
That's what it was, it was an insurance policy in the even

00:47:43.489 --> 00:47:46.829
Armageddon was really happening, we wanted to survive.

00:47:46.849 --> 00:47:49.659
And it turns out we didn't actually need the capital.

00:47:49.679 --> 00:47:52.400
Armageddon did not happen.

00:47:52.420 --> 00:47:54.420
Deepest recession of my lifetime happened, but not

00:47:54.339 --> 00:47:57.769
Armageddon.

00:47:57.789 --> 00:47:58.789
But, you know what?

00:47:58.609 --> 00:48:01.329
Leonard Green's been a great investor.

00:48:01.349 --> 00:48:05.389
They own 17% of the company and they don't control it, but

00:48:05.409 --> 00:48:07.409
they've been very valuable on the board.

00:48:07.269 --> 00:48:10.019
They do take a long term perspective, they love us.

00:48:10.039 --> 00:48:13.000
I guess because, we've already made like 500 million dollars

00:48:13.019 --> 00:48:14.269
for them in the year.

00:48:14.269 --> 00:48:15.269
[Laughter]

00:48:14.269 --> 00:48:19.039
John: So, one of my jokes is that when the stock is up I'm

00:48:19.059 --> 00:48:20.690
this visionary.

00:48:20.710 --> 00:48:23.710
And when the stock is down, I'm like the village idiot.

00:48:22.969 --> 00:48:23.969
[Laughter]

00:48:22.969 --> 00:48:27.159
John: Your reputation waxes and wanes based on the stock

00:48:27.179 --> 00:48:28.179
price.

00:48:28.179 --> 00:48:31.429
But with Leonard Green, I'm a visionary cuz they've made so

00:48:30.869 --> 00:48:31.869
much money.

00:48:31.869 --> 00:48:33.880
So, glad we did it.

00:48:33.900 --> 00:48:37.400
It was an insurance policy that I'm glad we didn't have to

00:48:36.400 --> 00:48:37.400
collect on.

00:48:37.400 --> 00:48:38.989
How's that?

00:48:39.009 --> 00:48:41.009
And I would -- But I think they are.

00:48:40.909 --> 00:48:45.799
You hear lots of bad things about private equity firms.

00:48:45.819 --> 00:48:49.589
Leonard Green is an outstanding firm with great integrity.

00:48:49.609 --> 00:48:51.609
I'm proud to be associated with them.

00:48:51.069 --> 00:48:52.819
Hope that gets printed in the newspaper.

00:48:52.819 --> 00:48:53.819
[Laughter]

00:48:52.819 --> 00:48:55.579
John: Thanks very much.

00:48:55.599 --> 00:48:56.599
[Applause]

00:48:55.599 --> 00:48:56.599
John: Thank You

